## Build an AI Voice Assistant with Twilio Voice and Media Streams, Rime's Prism, and Node


We're so excited for our friends at[Rime](https://www.rime.ai/prism) for their[release of Prism](https://www.rime.ai/prism) , their new speech-to-speech model for voice AI agents.


In this tutorial, we'll show you how to connect Prism to Twilio's voice infrastructure using[Twilio Media Streams](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams) , Node.js, and Rime's Node SDK. When you're done, you'll be able to call a phone number and talk to an AI voice assistant – complete with interruption handling and a tool call. After that, we’ll show you how to persist customer details with Twilio’s[Conversation Memory](https://www.twilio.com/docs/conversations/memory) , and analyze transcripts with[Conversation Intelligence](https://www.twilio.com/docs/conversations/intelligence) .


*So who you gonna call 👻?* Buckle up… let's get started!


## Prerequisites


Every good tutorial has a few prerequisites – to follow along with *this* tutorial, you will first need:


- Node.js 22+ (We used 22.12.0 for testing this tutorial; you can download it from[here](https://nodejs.org/) )
- **A Twilio account.** If you don't have one yet,[sign up for a free trial here](https://www.twilio.com/try-twilio) .
- A Twilio number with **Voice** capabilities.[Here are instructions](https://help.twilio.com/articles/223135247-How-to-Search-for-and-Buy-a-Twilio-Phone-Number-from-Console) if you don't have one yet.
- **A Rime account and a Rime API key.** You can create a key in the Rime dashboard under[API Tokens](https://app.rime.ai/tokens) .
- **ngrok or another tunneling solution** to expose your local server to the internet for testing.[You can download ngrok here](https://ngrok.com/) .
- **A cell phone or landline** that can make an outgoing phone call, and a willingness to talk to the agent that picks up!


Got all that? Then you're ready… let's build.


## Set up the Prism speech-to-speech Node project


Although you just ticked through the prerequisites, we still need to cover a few scaffolding and setup steps before we can build in earnest. In these next steps, we'll walk you through setting up your project, installing dependencies, and the code you'll need to connect Twilio and Prism.


More of a clone-and-start fan? Alternatively, you can[find our repository here](https://github.com/pkamp3/twilio-rime-prism-node/) .


Alright, let's *really* get started!


### Step 1: Initialize the Project


First, set up a new Node.js project. From the command line, run:


Copy code


```text
mkdir twilio-rime-prism-node
cd twilio-rime-prism-node
npm init -y; npm pkg set type="module";
```


### Step 2: Install dependencies


Next, you need to install the required dependencies for the project. We'll be[using the web framework Fastify](https://fastify.dev/) , a great option for a Node framework with WebSocket support. And we will store our sensitive environment variable in a` .env` file (You can find more information on this strategy[here](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/working-with-environment-variables-in-node-js-html) ).


We'll also install Rime's SDK,` @rimelabs/sdk` .


Copy code


```text
npm install fastify dotenv @fastify/formbody @fastify/websocket @rimelabs/sdk
```


### Step 3: Create the project files


Next, we will create three files:` .env` for our secrets,` index.js` for the server,` audio.js` for converting audio between Twilio and Prism. (There's a bit more on why we need that in a moment.)


#### Step 3.1: Create the .env File


First, create the` .env` file:


Copy code


```text
touch .env
```


Then – in your favorite text editor ([ed 's our favorite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ed_%28text_editor%29) ) – add your` RIME_API_KEY` on the first line:


Copy code


```text
RIME_API_KEY="your_rime_api_key_here"
```


The Rime SDK reads` RIME_API_KEY` on its own, so you won't see us pass it anywhere in the following code.


#### Step 3.2: Create the index.js and audio.js Files


Next, create the two code files in your project directory:


Copy code


```text
touch index.js audio.js
```


### Step 4: Write the audio conversion code


There's one wrinkle when connecting a phone call between Prism and Twilio's Media Streams: they don't speak the same audio format.


- [Twilio Media Streams sends and receives](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams/websocket-messages#start-message) 8 kHz[G.711 μ-law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G.711) .
- [Prism takes 16 kHz PCM16 in and sends 24 kHz PCM16 out](https://docs.rime.ai/docs/prism/overview) . It doesn't accept or send μ-law.


So on the way in, we need to decode μ-law to PCM16, while Rime's SDK resamples it for Prism. On the way out, we need to drop from 24 kHz down to 8 kHz for Media Streams.


Our algorithm is relatively simple: run the audio through a low-pass filter with a 3.6 kHz cutoff – which removes nearly everything at 4 kHz and above – then keep every third sample.


Paste the following code in your` audio.js` :


Copy code


```text
// Audio conversion between Twilio Media Streams (8 kHz G.711 mu-law) and Rime Prism (PCM16).
// Decode mu-law bytes to PCM16 samples. From Rime's docs.
export function mulawToPcm16(mulaw) {
const pcm = Buffer.alloc(mulaw.length * 2);
mulaw.forEach((value, i) => {
const byte = ~value & 0xff;
const magnitude = (((byte & 0x0f) << 3) + 0x84) << ((byte >> 4) & 0x07);
pcm.writeInt16LE(byte & 0x80 ? 0x84 - magnitude : magnitude - 0x84, i * 2);
});
return pcm;
}
// Encode one PCM16 sample as a mu-law byte (the reverse of mulawToPcm16).
function encodeMulaw(sample) {
const sign = sample < 0 ? 0x80 : 0;
const magnitude = Math.min(Math.abs(sample), 32635) + 0x84;
const exponent = 24 - Math.clz32(magnitude); // Which of mu-law's 8 loudness ranges the sample is in
const mantissa = (magnitude >> (exponent + 3)) & 0x0f;
return ~(sign | (exponent << 4) | mantissa) & 0xff;
}
// Low-pass filter for going from 24 kHz to 8 kHz. It keeps the 300-3,400 Hz phone voice band
// (ITU-T G.712), cuts off at 3.6 kHz, and removes nearly everything 8 kHz audio can't carry.
const TAPS = 95;
const FILTER = Array.from({ length: TAPS }, (_, i) => {
const n = i - (TAPS - 1) / 2;
const cutoff = 3600 / 24000;
const sinc = n === 0 ? 2 * cutoff : Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * cutoff * n) / (Math.PI * n);
return sinc * (0.54 - 0.46 * Math.cos((2 * Math.PI * i) / (TAPS - 1)));
});
// Convert 24 kHz PCM16 chunks to 8 kHz mu-law.
export function createReplyEncoder() {
let history = new Float64Array(TAPS - 1); // The end of the previous chunk
return (pcm24k) => {
const pcm = Buffer.from(pcm24k.buffer, pcm24k.byteOffset, pcm24k.byteLength);
const input = new Float64Array(history.length + pcm.length / 2);
input.set(history);
for (let i = history.length; i < input.length; i++) input[i] = pcm.readInt16LE((i - history.length) * 2);
// Each output sample filters TAPS input samples.
const mulaw = Buffer.alloc(Math.max(0, Math.floor((input.length - TAPS) / 3) + 1));
for (let out = 0; out < mulaw.length; out++) {
let sum = 0;
for (let t = 0; t < TAPS; t++) sum += FILTER[t] * input[out * 3 + t];
mulaw[out] = encodeMulaw(Math.round(sum));
}
history = input.subarray(mulaw.length * 3);
return mulaw;
};
}
```


Here's what the functions do:


- ` mulawToPcm16` decodes μ-law to PCM16. This decoder comes from[Rime's docs](https://docs.rime.ai/docs/prism/live-conversations#phone-audio) .
- ` encodeMulaw` converts one PCM16 sample into a μ-law byte.
- ` FILTER` is a low-pass filter. It keeps the 300–3,400 Hz telephone voice band (from[ITU-T G.712](https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-G.712) ), cuts off at 3.6 kHz, and removes (nearly) everything at 4 kHz and above.
- ` createReplyEncoder` keeps every third sample, leaving us with 8 kHz μ-law that Twilio can play.


### Step 5: Write the code


Here is a brief overview of the main components that will make up the AI voice assistant system we’ll be building.


- **Twilio Voice and Media Streams** : Handles the phone call and streams audio via WebSockets.
- **Node.js Server** : Your code and orchestration: manages the webhook, handles media conversion, connects to Rime, and includes any needed business logic.
- **Rime Prism** : Provides the speech-to-speech AI responses.


Now, let's walk through building each part of the application step by step.


#### Step 5.1: Import dependencies, load our Environment Variable, and initialize Fastify


We start by importing required modules and loading the environment variables from our` .env` file.


Paste the following code in your` index.js` :


Copy code


```text
import Fastify from 'fastify';
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
import fastifyFormBody from '@fastify/formbody';
import fastifyWs from '@fastify/websocket';
import { Rime, runConversation } from '@rimelabs/sdk';
import { createReplyEncoder, mulawToPcm16 } from './audio.js';
dotenv.config();
if (!process.env.RIME_API_KEY) {
console.error('Missing Rime API key. Please set it in the .env file.');
process.exit(1);
}
```


#### Step 5.2: Define some constants


Next, we define constants for the voice, the server port, the assistant's first line, and its instructions.


Here's what you should paste next in your file:


Copy code


```text
const VOICE = 'astra';
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5050;
// Send Twilio a mark at least every 100 ms of audio. Our rough benchmarks were ~300 ms per word, so
// this interval is around 1/3 of a word.
const MARK_INTERVAL_MS = 100;
const OPENING = "Ahoy! I'm an AI voice assistant powered by Twilio and Rime's Prism. How can I help?";
const INSTRUCTIONS = "You are an AI voice assistant powered by Twilio and Rime's Prism, and on a live phone call. "
+ 'Everything you say is spoken to the caller. Use plain spoken sentences, with no markdown, lists, or symbols. '
+ 'Spell out all numbers, for example "twenty-three". Answer in one to three short sentences. '
+ 'You are cheerful, with a penchant for dad jokes, owl jokes, and slipping an 80s song lyric into an answer. '
+ 'Never say it is a lyric or name the song, but you can joke about it. '
+ 'You want everyone to answer the phone with "ahoy". '
+ 'Call get_current_time before you answer any question about the current time or date.';
```


` VOICE` controls how the assistant will sound. Prism has[four voices](https://docs.rime.ai/docs/prism/sessions#choose-a-voice) :` astra` (the default),` aurora` ,` brando` , and` wawona` .


` PORT` controls which port your application will open. We'll discuss this a little more in the ngrok section below.


` MARK_INTERVAL_MS` controls how often we ask Twilio to confirm what the caller has heard. We'll explain why that matters in Step 5.5.


` OPENING` is your assistant's first spoken line.


` INSTRUCTIONS` is the system prompt. You might think a speech-to-speech model wouldn't need rules about markdown – but Prism speaks every word it writes, and without guidance, it can answer like a chat assistant, with lists and math notation. Rime recommends starting your instructions with rules for speech, so we do.


*(Song lyrics without a song name? Wonder if you can find those anywhere else. And*[Alexander Graham Bell wanted everyone to say "ahoy"](https://www.twilio.com/docs/glossary/what-is-ahoy) *–* *so does your voice agent.)*


#### Step 5.3: Define a tool


Prism can ask your backend to run a *tool* , sometimes known as *function calling* . To show a full tool round, we'll give the assistant one simple tool: the current time.


Paste this next:


Copy code


```text
const TIME_TOOL = {
name: 'get_current_time',
description: 'Get the current date and time in a time zone.',
parameters: {
type: 'object',
properties: { timezone: { type: 'string', description: 'An IANA time zone, for example America/New_York' } },
},
};
// Mock tool call. Add your own tool logic here.
const getCurrentTime = ({ timezone = 'America/Los_Angeles' }) => {
console.log('Synchronizing watches...');
return { timezone, now: new Date().toLocaleString('en-US', { timeZone: timezone, dateStyle: 'full', timeStyle: 'short' }) };
};
```


` TIME_TOOL` is the definition for Prism: a name, a description, and a JSON Schema for the arguments. The *description* is how Prism decides whether to call the tool – you need to write it the way you'd write documentation for a colleague who can't read your code.


` getCurrentTime` is our tool. It's a mock, of course, and straightforward to demo – swap in your own business logic here later!


#### Step 5.4: Define two routes


Now, let's get to the heart of the code. We create our Rime client, then define a root route (more of a health check…) and a route to handle incoming calls. The` /incoming-call` route will[return TwiML, Twilio's Markup Language](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/twiml) , to direct Twilio how to handle an incoming call.


Paste this into` index.js` after the tool:


Copy code


```text
const rime = new Rime();
const fastify = Fastify();
fastify.register(fastifyFormBody);
fastify.register(fastifyWs);
fastify.get('/', async () => ({ message: 'Twilio Media Stream Server is running!' }));
// Some tunnels rewrite the Host header. Set PUBLIC_HOST if you use one.
fastify.all('/incoming-call', async (request, reply) => {
const host = process.env.PUBLIC_HOST || request.headers['x-forwarded-host'] || request.headers.host;
reply.type('text/xml').send(`<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Response><Connect><Stream url="wss://${host}/media-stream" /></Connect></Response>`);
});
```


Our TwiML is short: a[<Connect>](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/twiml/connect) with a[<Stream>](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/twiml/stream) . This opens a[bidirectional Media Stream](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams) to our` /media-stream` route. We don't need a` <Say>` greeting this time, since Prism greets the caller.


` PUBLIC_HOST` is optional here, because some tunnels rewrite the` Host` header. If you use one of those, set` PUBLIC_HOST` in your` .env` to your tunnel's host name. Otherwise, the caller will hear dead air.


#### Step 5.5: Connect the call audio to Prism, and allow us to interrupt


This is the biggest step, so it's reproduced here in two pieces. Rime's SDK runs the conversation with a function called` runConversation` . We give the SDK two adapters: a *source* to read the caller's audio from, and *playback* to send Prism's replies to.


First, the source. Paste this next:


Copy code


```text
// Queue the caller's audio from Twilio until Prism needs it.
function createCallerAudio() {
const frames = [];
let waiting = null; // A read() waiting for the next frame
return {
push(frame) {
if (waiting) waiting(frame);
else frames.push(frame);
},
read(signal) {
if (frames.length) return Promise.resolve(frames.shift());
return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
const abort = () => reject(signal.reason);
signal.addEventListener('abort', abort, { once: true });
waiting = (frame) => {
waiting = null;
signal.removeEventListener('abort', abort);
resolve(frame);
};
});
},
};
}
```


Twilio pushes the caller's audio to us as fast as it can deliver it. Rime’s SDK reads it when it's ready.


Next, the playback. This one's a bit longer – we'll explain it after the code block:


Copy code


```text
// Play Prism's replies on the call. Twilio doesn't report how much audio has played, so we use a mark message.
// When Twilio sends a markback, we can infer the caller has heard that much.
function createTwilioPlayback(twilio, streamSid) {
const send = (message) => twilio.send(JSON.stringify({ streamSid, ...message }));
const replies = new Map(); // Replies still playing
let nextId = 0;
const finish = (reply, heardMs) => { replies.delete(reply.id); reply.resolve(heardMs); };
return {
// Prism started a reply. The SDK writes the reply's audio to the object we return.
open(ref) {
const reply = { id: String(nextId++), ref, sentMs: 0, heardMs: 0, ...Promise.withResolvers() };
replies.set(reply.id, reply);
const encode = createReplyEncoder();
return {
write(chunk) {
if (!replies.has(reply.id)) return; // The caller interrupted this reply.
const mulaw = encode(chunk.data);
const step = MARK_INTERVAL_MS * 8; // 8 kHz mu-law is 8 bytes per millisecond.
for (let start = 0; start < mulaw.length; start += step) {
const piece = mulaw.subarray(start, start + step);
send({ event: 'media', media: { payload: piece.toString('base64') } });
reply.sentMs += piece.length / 8;
send({ event: 'mark', mark: { name: `${reply.id}:${reply.sentMs}` } });
}
},
// Prism sent the last of this reply. When this mark comes back, the caller heard all of it.
end() {
if (replies.has(reply.id)) send({ event: 'mark', mark: { name: `${reply.id}:end` } });
},
// Resolves with how much of the reply the caller heard.
completion: () => reply.promise,
};
},
// The caller talked over these replies. Empty Twilio's buffer and report what the caller heard.
interrupt(refs) {
const stopped = [...replies.values()].filter((reply) => refs.includes(reply.ref));
if (stopped.length) send({ event: 'clear' });
for (const reply of stopped) {
console.log(`Interrupted after ${Math.round(reply.heardMs)} of ${Math.round(reply.sentMs)} ms`);
finish(reply, reply.heardMs);
}
},
// The conversation ended. Resolve every reply that's still playing.
stop() {
for (const reply of replies.values()) finish(reply, reply.heardMs);
},
// Twilio played everything up to this mark.
onMark(name) {
const [id, position] = name.split(':');
const reply = replies.get(id);
if (reply && position === 'end') finish(reply, reply.sentMs);
else if (reply) reply.heardMs = Number(position);
},
};
}
```


Playing audio is the most straightforward part:` write` converts a chunk (with our encoder from` audio.js` ) and sends it to Twilio as a[media message](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams/websocket-messages#send-a-media-message) .


##### Step 5.5.1: An interruption


Let us interrupt for a second to point out a tricky part of the code – uhh, *interruptions* . The interesting part when handling interruptions is *knowing what the caller heard* . When a caller talks over a reply, Prism wants to know how much of that reply they heard. But Twilio doesn't report where playback is, it just faithfully receives and sends audio chunks.


So, to handle it, we use[Media Streams mark messages](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams/websocket-messages#send-a-mark-message) . After each 100 ms of audio, we send a reply mark and how many milliseconds we've sent so far... for example,` 3:1200` . Twilio handles the marks and audio *serially* , that is, it sends each mark back after the audio before it has played. Using the returned marks, we can estimate how much the caller heard (I mean, within the bounds of some additional latency and network effects. And, you know, *also* within 100ms.).


Here's what each method does:


- ` open` : Prism started a reply. We set up its bookkeeping and return an object for the SDK to write the reply's audio.
- ` write` : Converts the audio, sends it to Twilio in 100 ms pieces, and sends a mark after each piece.
- ` end` : Prism sent the last of this reply. We send a final` end` mark.
- ` completion` : Resolves with our estimate of *how much* of the reply the caller heard.
- ` interrupt` : The caller talked over a reply ('interrupted'). We send Twilio a[clear message](https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams/websocket-messages#send-a-clear-message) to empty its buffer, then report how much the caller heard.
- ` stop` : The conversation ended, so we resolve any reply that's still playing.
- ` onMark` : Twilio played everything up to this mark. We update how much the caller heard. (After a` clear` , Twilio also returns any marks it was still holding. By then, we've already finished that reply, so we ignore them.)


#### Step 5.6: Run tools and log the conversation


Next, we need to handle Prism's events. Paste this next:


Copy code


```text
// Run the tool, then ask Prism to continue its reply with the result.
async function runTool(session, { call, name, arguments: args }) {
console.log('Tool call:', name, args);
let result;
try {
result = name === TIME_TOOL.name ? getCurrentTime(args) : { error: 'unknown tool' };
} catch {
result = { error: `${name} failed` };
}
await session.submitToolResult(call, JSON.stringify(result));
await session.continueReply(call);
}
// Log the conversation. The SDK needs this function to return right away.
function onEvent(session, { payload }) {
switch (payload.kind) {
case 'speech.started':
return console.log('Caller started speaking');
case 'transcript.final':
return console.log('Caller:', payload.text);
case 'text.done':
return console.log('Assistant:', payload.text);
case 'tool.call':
return void runTool(session, payload)
.catch((error) => console.log('Tool round ended early:', error.fault?.code ?? error.message));
case 'error':
return console.error('Prism error:', payload.error.code, payload.error.message);
}
}
```


` onEvent` logs to your console: you will see when the caller starts speaking, what the caller said, and what the assistant said. The SDK calls this function for every event, and it must be synchronous – so we don't wait for tools here.


When Prism asks for a tool,` runTool` runs it. It then gives Prism the result with` submitToolResult` , and asks Prism to continue its reply with` continueReply` . If the caller talks during a tool call, Prism answers the caller instead and drops the tool round – so` continueReply` throws, and we log it.


#### Step 5.7: Open a Prism session for each call


Now, we tie everything together. Paste this next:


Copy code


```text
// Open one Prism session for the call, greet the caller, and run the conversation.
async function converse(callerAudio, playback) {
const session = await rime.realtime.connect({ voice: VOICE, instructions: INSTRUCTIONS, tools: [TIME_TOOL] });
// Quote this ID if you ever need Rime's help with a call.
console.log('Prism session', session.info.sessionId);
session.requestReply({ instruction: `Say, word for word: "${OPENING}"` })
.catch((error) => console.error('Greeting failed:', error.message));
await runConversation(session, { source: callerAudio, playback, onEvent: (event) => onEvent(session, event) });
}
```


` converse` opens one Prism session for the call with our choice of voice (hey, that Rimes rhymes!), instructions, and tool. It logs the session ID – keep it handy if you ever need Rime's help with a call. Then it asks Prism to greet the caller with` requestReply` , and hands off to` runConversation` .


#### Step 5.8: Handle the Media Stream


Time to switch sides! Next, we set up the WebSocket route for the Media Stream – the route we give Twilio in our TwiML.


Paste this next:


Copy code


```text
fastify.register(async (fastify) => {
fastify.get('/media-stream', { websocket: true }, (connection) => {
console.log('Client connected');
const callerAudio = createCallerAudio();
let playback;
connection.on('message', (message) => {
// One malformed message must not crash the server and drop every other call.
let data;
try {
data = JSON.parse(message);
} catch {
console.error('Ignoring a message that is not JSON');
return;
}
try {
switch (data.event) {
case 'start':
if (playback) return; // One conversation per stream
console.log('Incoming stream has started', data.start.streamSid);
playback = createTwilioPlayback(connection, data.start.streamSid);
// When the Prism session ends, close the stream. Twilio then hangs up.
converse(callerAudio, playback)
.catch((error) => console.error('Conversation failed:', error))
.finally(() => connection.close());
break;
// Forward every frame, including silence.
case 'media':
callerAudio.push({
data: mulawToPcm16(Buffer.from(data.media.payload, 'base64')),
format: { sampleRate: 8000 },
});
break;
case 'mark':
playback?.onMark(data.mark.name);
break;
}
} catch (error) {
console.error(`Bad ${data?.event} message:`, error.message);
}
});
connection.on('close', () => {
callerAudio.push(null);
console.log('Client disconnected.');
});
});
});
```


Here's how we handle each Twilio message:


- ` start` : The stream has started. We create the playback for this call's stream (` createTwilioPlayback` ) and start the conversation. When the Prism session ends, we close the stream, and Twilio hangs up.
- ` media` : The caller's audio. We decode it and pass it to Prism (including silence, which Prism listens for to estimate when the caller is done).
- ` mark` : Twilio played a mark we sent, so pass it to the playback.
- When the WebSocket closes, tell the SDK the call ended.
- We also have two safety checks: a message that isn't JSON is logged and skipped. Also, a second` start` is ignored (so one stream never opens two Prism sessions).


#### Step 5.9: Prepare the server


And finally, we start the Fastify server and bring it all home (using the port we passed in or defined). Paste this below what you have, and we're ready to roll! Or, you know, dial!


Copy code


```text
fastify.listen({ port: PORT }, (err) => {
if (err) {
console.error(err);
process.exit(1);
}
console.log(`Server is listening on port ${PORT}`);
});
```


#### Run the server


Can't you hear the server saying *"Start Me Up?"* – you can now run the server with the following command:


Copy code


```text
node index.js
```


If the server starts successfully, you should see the message` Server is listening on port 5050` (or, alternatively, the port you specified) in your terminal.


### Step 6. Finish the basic integration


Now it's time to provide instructions to Twilio and finish the wiring! We'll next cover using the reverse proxy` ngrok` to make your server publicly accessible.


#### Step 6.1: Expose your server using ngrok


Now, you need to use ngrok or a similar service to expose your local server to the public internet. Twilio requires a public URL to send requests to your server and receive instructions from you.


We'll provide instructions for ngrok. You can[find other reverse proxy or tunneling options here](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/expose-localhost-to-internet-with-tunnel) .


Download and install ngrok if you still need to, then run the following command. And if you have changed the port from 5050, remember to update it here:


Copy code


```text
ngrok http 5050
```


